A||||6||Bill H||modified or stock motor?||||5||
Z||000000||Bill H||11-19-2000||09:40 PM||||I have an '88 Fiero Formula and am looking for some opinions as to whether or not I should put in a modified or stock motor in it.  From a collectors perspective a stock motor may be prefered but from a speed perspective a modified motor may be prefered.  What do you reccomend?<BR>   I concidered a 3.8 swap but I would have to destroy the stock harness. If I went with the 3.4 with performance heads and cam FOCCA headers I could come close in performance and rebuild the stock motor for an occasion where the stock car would be worth more. I plain to keep the car for years but as  time goes on they start to get rarer and rarer. If I destroy the harness or any other parts now I might not find replacement parts in 5-10 years. Any thoughts would be listened too carefully Thanks<p>[This message has been edited by Bill H (edited 11-19-2000).]||||reg||5
Z||000001||jerpro||11-19-2000||10:06 PM||||hmmmm.. for what it's going to cost you, why don't you look around for a fiero with a shot motor and use that one for the v8swap and keep the one you have stock?<P><P>------------------<BR>Only try your best, and if you don't succeed... TRY HARDER.||||reg||1
Z||000002||WKDFIRO||11-20-2000||06:53 AM||||I still have to disagree that the Fiero is going to become the magical mystical "Collector's Car" that some people talk about. Whether it will be worth more stock than custom is debateable to say the least. More likely the person that is going to appreciate it the most is you. <P>Keeping it stock should be a decision that you make based on what you want out of the car. Not the profit.<P>The only distinction that 88s do have is the scarcity of parts. Fiero parts will get rare but because most of the parts of the 88, namely the suspension, didn't come out of the parts bin it will be harder still.<P>Whatever you decide to do with it, just have fun with it!||||reg||5
Z||000003||Old Lar||11-20-2000||07:06 AM||||I agree with WK.  A used Fiero will not become a collector car unless it has low, low milage or has had extensive restoration.<BR>You need to determine which route you want to do, extensive restoration back to stock and have the car in storage to prevent paint chips or rebuild the car as a driving experience.  The car will always get looks because it is becoming rare.  A car as a mode of investment is a poor choice.  Even a 20 year old pristine Corvette is worth maybe 20% over the original sticker price.  Not a real good return on invetment.  Also a car is worth what any one is willing to pay for it.  So you'd need to find someone really wanting a Fiero and willing to pay big bucks to make money.||||reg||3
Z||000004||theogre||11-20-2000||07:48 PM||||Actually most fully restored cars end up selling for less than what they cost to restore. There are exceptions to that but not a whole lot. Has to be pretty rare or special in some way to begin with most of the time.||||reg||1
Z||000005||MikeE||11-20-2000||08:50 PM||||I agree with WKDFIRO, OLD LAR, and theogre.  I doubt if Fieros are ever going to be worth much.  Collectability requires both rarity and desirablity.  Fieros aren't that rare and are only desirable to us, a small dedicated group.  Right now it takes far more money to restore them than what they are worth.  By the time they are worth anything we will all be dead and the restoration costs will be astronomical due to lack of parts.<P>My point is that due to the high cost of any restoration vs. its low return, you have to really like the way the car is stock to go that route.  The big advantage of Fieros is that they are still cheap enough to buy and have fun with.  Once a car becomes collectable, it is too expensive initially for a modified project car but too expensive too restore unless you are in love with it in its stock form.<P>Therefore, whether or not to modify the Formula depends on how much you have in it.  It might be cheaper to buy a car with a shot motor to work on, then sell the Formula to recoupe some of your costs.  That way, you would have a Fiero to drive while you're doing your project.  This only works if you have the extra room and cash.  Otherwise you could sell the Formula and buy a cheap project car with money left over for mods.  If you want to stick with an 88, however, I've noticed that many people want nearly as much for an 88 coupe as for 88 Formulas or GTs which doesn't make sense to me.  I guess mechanically shot 88 coupes are hard to find (but I'm looking).<P><BR>------------------<BR><p>[This message has been edited by MikeE (edited 11-20-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000006||Cooter||11-20-2000||10:28 PM||||Cheap 88's are around. My Fiero partner just bought one a few weeks ago for $150. It has a bad motor, but the receipts in the console show where $500 worth of AC work had just been done. It will eventually make its way to the Fiero graveyard  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif"><BR>BTW- I have an 88 GT that I have modded the crap out of! 3.4, 4 speed, Camaro seats, Indy scoop, z-28 scoop on front, soon to have a big turbo and Sunfire GT 16 inch wheels. Make it YOUR car, one like no one else can have and enjoy it!||||reg||1
